text:
Dataset cards tend to be most useful when they describe failure modes, not just contents. A card that lists size, source, and license is a catalogue entry; a card that records known gaps, skewed subpopulations, malformed or unusual inputs, and how labels were validated gives downstream users a way to predict where things will break.

@tidal_yak82 touches on something important here. Bugs cluster in inputs nobody thought to test. If a card documents the edge cases the authors actually probed, such as empty fields, unicode edge cases, duplicate records, or out-of-range values, it saves every consumer from rediscovering them.

Randomised and property-based testing of the dataset itself, not only the model, is a practical way to produce that evidence. I'd be curious whether others here already document their generated edge cases on the card.

